3. TWO CORRECTIONS TO CARRY FORWARD
================================================================================

3.1  recommended_version_id WAS REMOVED IN ERROR AND MUST BE RESTORED
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

docs/dbspec01 states:

    For each repository asset:

        recommended_version_id

    is nullable, so:

        NULL = no approved/recommended version

It was removed in Phase 0 (commit 5d3d24f). The reasoning given at the time was
that a single pointer cannot express "an asset can hold several production
versions at once", so release membership should carry the meaning instead.

That reasoning was WRONG. "Recommended" and "release membership" are different
things:

    recommended_version_id   which version to build from NOW    (a pointer, one)
    release membership       what shipped, and when             (history, many)

Both coexist. Phase 0 also left `asset_recommended_versions` (the companion
table) in place while deleting the column, so the live schema is currently in a
state neither the spec nor the code describes.

Current state, verified 2026-10-23:

    live assets columns : id, name, asset_type, category, description,
                          created_at, updated_at, is_active
    db.js declares it   : no — the COLUMN is not restored, and that is now a
                          DECISION rather than an omission. See below.
    asset_recommended_versions table : DECLARED in db.js (db.js:356) and
                          present in the live schema. Restored 2026-10-09.
                          Nothing writes it yet.

RESTORING THE COLUMN IS NO LONGER THE PLAN. Item 78 decided the opposite, and
this note originally pointed the other way: the TABLE does the same job and
constrains it, because its composite foreign key proves the recommended version
belongs to this asset. A bare integer column cannot say that, and a pointer to
another asset's version is a state nothing would catch. Two mechanisms for one
fact would be worse than either.

(The snapshot above was also stale in a smaller way — it omitted `category`,
which was added by ALTER. Corrected with the rest, 2026-10-23.)

3.2  YOU CANNOT RUN AN UNREAL GAME IN A BROWSER
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

"Run a cutdown version of the game so the assets can be seen on the website"
is not achievable with Unreal Engine:

  * UE's HTML5 target was dropped after UE4. The WASM/WebGPU work is
    experimental with no shipping path.
  * Arbitrary .uasset/.umap files cannot be hot-loaded into a browser build.
    UE assets are cooked into the binary.

Note the spec does NOT ask for this — 02.4 says "WebGL viewer", which is a
different and reachable thing. Three real options:

  Option                  What the approver sees            Cost
  ----------------------  --------------------------------  -----------------
  Pre-rendered turntables Highest fidelity; exactly the    Offline render job.
  (UE Movie Render Queue  game's look. Not interactive.    No runtime infra.
   -> image sequence)

  glTF + three.js with    Interactive orbit/zoom.          Cheap once assets
  art-directed lighting   Approximate look.                are exported to GLB.

  Pixel Streaming         LITERALLY the game in a browser. PERSISTENT GPU
  (real UE on a GPU host,                                  SERVER. Directly
   streamed to browser)                                    contradicts 05.

Pixel Streaming is the only thing that delivers the literal request, and it is
the one the spec's own cost principle forbids. From 05:

    "To preserve workstation GPU hardware for Unreal Engine development and
     eliminate recurring idle server costs, all pipeline workloads are hosted
     on decoupled, pay-per-execution cloud infrastructure."

That is a direct trade-off and needs an explicit decision, not a default.

