================================================================================
5. PROPOSED BREAKDOWN
================================================================================

Ordered cheapest-first. Each layer is independently useful.

A. ASSET TYPE REGISTRY                                          SMALL
   Replace free-text asset_type with a controlled vocabulary:
   blend | nuke | unreal | maya | image | gltf.
   Per type: allowed extensions, required companions, and validation rules.
   Extend the technical-check suite with the spec's vetting-gate rules
   (polygon limits, texture dimensions, naming conventions, format compliance).
   This is where "upload types" actually lands.

B. DOWNLOAD TO LOCAL WORKING COPY                               SMALL
   ZIP preserving relative_path (which exists in the schema for exactly this)
   plus a JSON manifest: per-file SHA-256, size, and target path. The approver
   has a working copy of the game, so files must land in the right places.
   A small CLI later lets an artist sync by hash instead of re-downloading.
   HIGHEST VALUE, LOWEST RISK. Unblocks approval review immediately.
   SECURITY: this is the first code that serves user-uploaded bytes.
     - Use application/octet-stream + Content-Disposition: attachment.
       NEVER the stored mime_type, or an uploaded .svg/.html becomes stored
       XSS on this origin.
     - Resolve bytes only from stored_files.storage_key; validate it is exactly
       64 hex chars before touching the filesystem. Never build a path from
       client input.
     - Re-validate relative_path at zip time. It was normalised on upload, but
       it originated client-side and is now being used to build entry names.

C. PREVIEW, TIERED BY WHAT IS ACTUALLY RENDERABLE                MEDIUM
     images (.png/.jpg/.exr/.tga)  inline. Verify magic bytes; serve with a
                                   safe content type rather than the
                                   client-supplied one.
     .glb/.gltf                    three.js viewer. Real 3D, cheap.
     .blend/.nk/.ma/.uasset        none render in a browser. Options:
                                   artist-uploaded renders, offline conversion
                                   to GLB (Blender does this headlessly), or
                                   download-only for now.

D. THE "SEE IT IN THE GAME" LAYER                               LARGE
   Pick one of the three options in 3.2. If turntables: an OFFLINE render
   pipeline, not a web feature.

E. DISTRIBUTION VIA GIT                                         LARGE
   The spec's actual answer for 02.6. Partial plumbing exists in
   routes/superuser.js.

================================================================================
6. OPEN QUESTIONS
================================================================================

Q1. Cloud stack or local stack?
    Is this section targeting the spec's architecture (Cloudflare R2, RunPod,
    Qdrant, GitHub LFS) or the current local implementation (SQLite + local
    disk)? This decides everything else. Working assumption: local-first, with
    interfaces shaped so R2 can slot in later.

Q2. Which preview approach?
    Pixel Streaming, pre-rendered turntables, or three.js glTF? Genuinely
    different projects. Pixel Streaming additionally requires accepting a
    continuously running GPU server, against spec 05.

Q3. Which asset types first?
    Working assumption: images and GLB (both renderable), with .blend/.nk/
    .uasset treated as download-only until a conversion pipeline exists.

Q4. Restore recommended_version_id?
    Recommendation: yes, as part of this section. See 3.1.

================================================================================
7. RECOMMENDED STARTING POINT
================================================================================

Layer B. It is unambiguous, small, needed under every answer to Q1 and Q2, and
it is the one thing that immediately turns approval review from guesswork into
something real. Layers C and D are the parts that depend on Q2.

